Stone-Vowel Bruk
Ingredients: SARDINIAN CANTO A TENORE + BROKEN BEAT / BRUK + PREPARED CLAVINET + FRAME DRUM + BASS TRUMPET + DRY ANALOG SUB

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during an overnight acoustic survey inside a decommissioned granite-cutting workshop near Nuoro, Sardinia, where four traditional vocalists were testing close-circle canto a tenore resonance against bare stone walls while a London broken-beat producer programmed loose, syncopated drums in the former tool room. The fusion became coherent when the producer stopped sampling the voices as sustained pads and instead treated the quartet’s internal entries—bassu, contra, mesu boche, and boche—as four independent rhythmic layers. Prepared clavinet, frame drum, bass trumpet, and restrained mono sub were added to create a form where human overtone density and broken-beat swing occupy the same structural grid.

Stone-Vowel Bruk is a vocal-acoustic club form built around the physical interlock of Sardinian canto a tenore and the elastic timing of broken beat. The vocal quartet is not used as a cinematic choir or atmospheric drone. Its power comes from the distinct roles of the four singers: boche carrying the textual or melodic line, mesu boche shaping a higher supporting color, contra providing a rough middle overtone field, and bassu anchoring the lowest guttural resonance.

The voices are recorded in a tight inward-facing circle with one central omnidirectional microphone and four close spot microphones. The central mic preserves the fused body of the quartet and the way overtones combine in air; the spot mics preserve each singer’s onset, breath, consonants, formant grain, and timing. These signals remain naturalistic. Formant-shifting plugins, choir doubling, and synthetic throat layers are excluded from the core sound.

Broken beat supplies the machine architecture: syncopated kick placement, displaced snares, swung sixteenths, cross-rhythmic hi-hats, ghost percussion, and bass notes that frequently arrive between expected accents. The groove should feel elastic and conversational rather than quantized. Full drum loops are avoided in favor of deliberately written two- and four-bar cells.

The central compositional device is the Formant Relay. Each of the four vocal roles is assigned a rhythmic function for a section. Bassu may correspond to low-frequency entry, contra to offbeat percussion density, mesu boche to upper syncopation, and boche to melodic reset. When one singer enters or changes vowel color, another instrumental lane changes behavior rather than merely doubling the voice. A bassu entrance may open a sub note; a contra vowel shift may remove a hi-hat; a mesu boche call may trigger a clavinet response after the fact. The relationship is compositional, not sensor-driven.

The quartet favors short, repeatable vowel shapes, open fifth relationships, narrow modal cells, sustained pedal tones, clipped syllables, and compact call-response phrases. Long folkloric narrative singing is possible but not the default. The key requirement is that the separate vocal functions remain audible enough for the rhythm section to react to them.

Prepared clavinet provides dry, fixed-pitch punctuation. Felt strips, removable paper, soft rubber, and selective string muting shorten the instrument’s bark so it can answer vocal attacks without becoming a funk comping bed. Voicings favor open fifths, minor sevenths, suspended seconds, stacked fourths, and two-note chromatic approach figures.

Frame drum provides the live acoustic transient layer. Bass strokes, fingertip taps, rim strokes, muted slaps, and brushed hand rolls replace selected programmed hits rather than stacking under them. Its job is to introduce microtiming and skin texture without blurring the broken-beat grid.

Bass trumpet occupies the low-mid register with short held fifths, breath-heavy attacks, half-valve falls, muted swells, and restrained two-note calls. It enters mostly after vocal phrases, acting as a metallic continuation of the quartet’s resonance rather than a brass lead.

Analog sub remains mono, short, and highly selective. It reinforces only designated bassu moments or low clavinet anchors. Continuous sub drones are avoided because the vocal quartet already creates strong low-mid density.

The defining tension is communal blend versus rhythmic separation. The singers are strongest when their tones fuse, but the arrangement depends on hearing exactly who enters, changes vowel, releases, or leaves space.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 118–128 BPM, usually 4/4 with broken-beat swing, displaced kicks, clipped snares, ghost percussion, frame-drum substitutions, and strong 3-over-4 implications without changing the underlying meter.
- Harmonic Palette: Dorian, Aeolian, open fifths, modal thirds, minor 7ths, suspended 2nds, stacked fourths, octave pedals, and occasional chromatic lower-neighbor movement.
- Production Signatures:
  1. Formant Relay: each vocal role is assigned a structural rhythmic function; instrument behavior changes when that role enters, shifts vowel, or releases.
  2. Circle-and-Spot Capture: one central room mic preserves fused quartet resonance while four close microphones keep individual timing and vocal grain legible.
  3. Skin-for-Snare Exchange: selected electronic snare, clap, or rim positions are replaced completely by dry frame-drum strokes.
  4. Clavinet Reply: prepared clavinet phrases arrive after key vocal attacks or vowel shifts rather than underneath them.
  5. Brass Residue: bass trumpet extends the low-mid tail after vocal release using short, breath-heavy responses.
  6. No-Choir Rule: no synthetic choir stacking, pitch-spread duplication, or formant plugins on the core vocal quartet.
- Dynamics: Intensity grows through denser vocal overlap, closer call-response spacing, tighter broken-beat syncopation, brighter clavinet attacks, stronger frame-drum substitutions, and shorter rests. Peaks remain dry, human, and groove-centered rather than cinematic.

Scene & Origin Lore
Stone-Vowel Bruk formed inside a retired granite-cutting workshop in the hills outside Nuoro. The building retained stone dust, scored worktables, steel saw frames, chalk measurements, brass angle gauges, stacked offcuts, old lifting hooks, blue-grey work lamps, and thick masonry walls that returned low voices with short, powerful reflections.

Lead boche singer Matteo Carta had been organizing informal acoustic tests with bassu singer Gavino Piras, contra singer Paolo Sanna, and mesu boche singer Luca Mureddu. They were interested in how close-circle spacing changed the apparent density of the quartet. London producer Amara Vale was in Sardinia for a rhythm residency and initially tried to sample one sustained chord. The loop sounded impressive but static.

Amara instead asked each singer to enter separately on the same tonal center. She built a broken-beat pattern that changed one lane at each entrance: kick after bassu, hat removal after contra, clavinet answer after mesu boche, and full pattern reset after boche. The quartet remained traditional in vocal production while the surrounding arrangement became radically modern.

Percussionist Giulia Serra joined with a large frame drum and insisted that her strokes replace machine hits rather than decorate them. Keyboardist Tomas Reed prepared a clavinet so its attacks could stay short and woody. Bass-trumpeter Elena Brooks completed the ensemble by adding low brass only after vocal release points.

Their first performances were called relay circles. The four singers stood in the center while the instrumentalists formed an outer ring. Small floor markers showed which vocal role controlled which rhythmic lane for that piece. Nothing was electronically triggered; the musicians watched and listened for the vocal changes.

The visual language developed around granite grey, chalk white, oxidized steel, dark wool, faded cobalt workwear, brass measuring tools, raw timber, and cold workshop light interrupted by a few amber maintenance lamps. The unofficial rule is that every layer must know which voice it is listening to.

