Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Bassu Enters Before the Dust Settles
- Story & Context: The debut opens at 124 BPM with only ghost hats and a clipped snare. Gavino enters on a low bassu drone before the previous workshop footstep noise has fully faded. Amara responds by introducing one short sub note and a displaced kick, but leaves the upper percussion sparse. Paolo’s contra arrives four bars later, causing the hats to disappear completely while Giulia replaces the next snare with one dry frame-drum slap. Luca’s mesu boche enters with a narrow higher vowel, answered one beat later by Tomas on a prepared-clavinet fourth. Matteo’s boche is the last voice to appear and resets the entire pattern into its fullest form. Midway through, the entry order reverses, forcing the groove to rebuild from the top voice downward. The track ends with bassu alone after every machine layer has stopped.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Fine granite dust suspended above a scored workbench while four handwritten vocal-role labels—BASSU, CONTRA, MESU BOCHE, BOCHE—form a circular diagram beside a compact drum grid. Sparse vocal text only: short Sardinian vowel calls, counting fragments, and the repeated English production cue “wait for the low voice.”

Track 2: Four Mouths Share One Offbeat
- Story & Context: At 126 BPM, all four singers attack the same tonal center but stagger their entries across one beat. Amara writes a broken-beat pattern that leaves that entire beat free of kick and snare, allowing the vocal stagger to become the primary rhythmic event. Giulia answers only the contra entry with a fingertip rim stroke. Tomas places a two-note clavinet minor seventh after the mesu boche release, while Elena adds one breath-heavy bass-trumpet fall after Matteo’s final consonant. Halfway through, the singers keep the same notes but switch vowel shape; the overtone blend brightens without changing harmony. Amara responds by darkening the hats and removing the sub entirely, making the quartet feel larger despite fewer electronic layers.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Four side-profile mouths arranged around a single chalk-drawn offbeat marker on rough granite, one frame-drum rim and one clavinet key visible at the edges, cold workshop light. Sparse lyrical fragments: “one beat, four entries,” “same stone,” “change the vowel,” and short open-vowel calls rather than full verses.

Track 3: The Saw Frame Hums After the Circle Breaks
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 120 BPM and begins with the quartet already blended in a sustained open fifth. Amara keeps the drums minimal: one kick, one snare, and small ghost notes. Every eight bars one singer steps physically backward from the circle, changing the acoustic blend before actually stopping. First mesu boche moves away, then contra, then bassu. The central microphone captures the fused tone becoming progressively thinner even while the close microphones remain stable. Tomas reduces his clavinet voicings in parallel, from two-note shapes to single muted notes. Elena shifts from pitched bass-trumpet responses to breath-only valve noise. In the final passage Matteo remains alone, sings one short phrase, and leaves the central mic. The last audible tone is not a voice but the faint sympathetic hum of an unused steel saw frame vibrating in the room.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty granite workshop after rehearsal, four circular floor marks separating from one central microphone, large steel saw frame in the background catching a faint vibration, chalk dust and dark cables under blue-grey work light. Sparse lyrical fragments: “break the circle,” “step back before silence,” “one voice left,” and “the frame keeps humming.”

